    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-14981",
      "problem": "Fill in the blanks so that the following reasoning is correct:\n\nThe notation $3 \\mid 18$ means ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14114596/assets/a23a0ff3469853645fc2df337ecd17b5ad3e7c7b35f7fc13dca55a1f5b6b4ee4.svg) It is equivalent to the statement that there exists \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14114596/assets/f0073ab043d4424f1e644949d00e03e53a23392f83a9c48e34924cfb2dafce5c.svg) such that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14114596/assets/905851beaba534389ff377cb1e30ca14d4774c5c047bdbb6e63c40f106576042.svg)",
      "worked_solution": "The notation $a \\mid b$ means $a$ divides $b.$ So, $3 \\mid 18$ means ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14114596/assets/eaa50ed893f4ba65ce4d7d3fd192e0024550a5712b06765c3c945cabae262bd9.svg)\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=a k.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $3 \\mid 18$ is equivalent to the statement that there exists $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{an integer}}$ $k$ such that $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}18 = 3k}.$",
